{"name":"Coffee / green tea ritual","deep":true,"image":null,"mentions":28,"casual":{"lead":["Users drink coffee or green tea as a morning routine meant to reduce facial puffiness. Both contain caffeine, and green tea adds polyphenols such as EGCG.","Caffeine blocks adenosine receptors and can briefly increase urine output after large single doses in people not used to it, which is the basis for the de-puffing idea. Regular users develop tolerance. A 2011 EFSA review found health claims for green tea polyphenols were not supported by sufficient evidence, and no study has tested either drink for facial puffiness."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["People have a cup of coffee or green tea in the morning, often alongside water and a low-salt breakfast. Some also use cooled tea bags or tea ice on the face. Effects are described as short-lived."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["Caffeine can disrupt sleep, and poor sleep can add to puffiness. Concentrated green tea extracts have been associated with liver injury."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[],"links":{"studies":[{"title":"Caffeinated Coffee Consumption or Abstinence to Reduce Atrial Fibrillation: The DECAF Randomized Clinical Trial.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41206802/","source":"JAMA","year":2026,"cites":null,"kind":"Randomized Controlled Trial"},{"title":"Coffee's Impact on Health and Well-Being.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40806142/","source":"Nutrients","year":2025,"cites":null,"kind":"Review"},{"title":"Coffee's protective mechanisms against neurodegeneration.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39168556/","source":"Progress in brain research","year":2024,"cites":null,"kind":"Review"},{"title":"The effect of caffeine on subsequent sleep: A systematic review and meta-analysis.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36870101/","source":"Sleep medicine reviews","year":2023,"cites":null,"kind":"Meta-Analysis"},{"title":"Coffee Consumption and Risk of Hypertension